  "method": "The two tanks (A and B) of IceTop stations 1 to 27, split into three records of 27 stations to stay within 400 vertices. Tank position = mean horizontal (x, y) of the two DOMs in the tank (DOMs 61, 62 are tank A, 63, 64 tank B of each station, in the GraphNeT copy of the IceCube IC86 geometry table, whose in-ice rows equal the toise geometry file to 2e-5 m). Frame and anchor: the IceCube coordinate system has its y-axis along the Prime Meridian (Grid North, towards Greenwich) and its x-axis 90 degrees clockwise from it (Grid East) (IceCube instrumentation paper, arXiv v3, section 5.3), and its origin is at elevation 883.9 m. The IceCube Collaboration software repository i3astropy gives the nominal location of the centre of the detector as lat -89.9944, lon -62.6081, height 883.9 m, which is taken as the frame origin. Conversion: the origin is placed at that point in a South Pole azimuthal-equidistant plane (distance from the pole = colatitude x 6 371 008.8 m, grid north = 0 deg meridian, grid east = 90 deg E; this is the same plane the browser draws), each string is the origin plus its (x, y) offset, and the inverse projection gives lat/lon. The origin is 622.7 m from the pole on the 62.6 deg W meridian. Each tank is drawn as a square of side 1.82 m, the inner diameter in the IceTop paper (Table 3), aligned to grid north, so it is a position marker with the real tank size, not the tank outline. Accuracy: the layout of the strings relative to each other is surveyed to about 1 m, and the pattern keeps its true size and orientation (grid north). The absolute position of the whole pattern is only good to about 500 m: the anchor is a nominal value of no stated epoch, the array moves with the ice about 10 m a year relative to geographic coordinates, and the lat/lon of IceCube on Wikipedia and in OpenStreetMap (89.99 S, 63.4531 W) lies 489 m from the anchor used. The tank source is a secondary copy of the collaboration geometry, so the shape stays derived; independent agreement with the IceTop paper is listed in the notes.",
  "name": "IceTop tanks, stations 1-27",
  "notes": [
    "Independent agreement with the IceTop paper (arXiv:1207.6326) over all 81 stations: tank pairs are a median 9.82 m apart against \"10 m apart\"; the pair midpoint is a median 24.5 m from its in-ice hole against \"placed nominally 25 m from the top of the corresponding in-ice hole\"; the lowest tank top is z = 1944.54 m (tank 18A) and the highest z = 1950.08 m (tank 74A), exactly the values the paper states; the two DOMs of a tank are 0.58 m apart as in Table 3.",
    "Stations 37, 40, 48, 50, 74 and 81 sit 36 m to 47 m from their hole and stations 21 and 57 about 17 m to 19 m: the paper says the irregularity arises because tank locations were constrained by surface cabling and drilling, and that station 81 is not next to a string."
  ],
